# storage

A disk is an array of bytes. Every structure below is a field inside those
bytes; no copy of the tree exists anywhere else. What sda carries is real
**ext2**: export the image and Linux's e2fsck, debugfs and mount -o loop
read it directly. Nothing on the disk is an invention of this machine.

## On-disk format

    sda: 1024 blocks of 1024 bytes, 1 MiB total; 256 inodes of 128 bytes

    byte 1024     superblock (s_inodes_count, s_blocks_count, s_magic 0xEF53)
    byte 2048     group descriptor table (where the bitmaps and table are)
    block 3       block bitmap, one bit per block, bit n is block n + 1
    block 4       inode bitmap, one bit per inode
    block 5..36   inode table, inode n sits at (n-1) * 128 inside it
    block 37..1023  data blocks

Every multi-byte field on the disk is little endian. The fields that matter
in an inode:

    0x00  i_mode    top 4 bits S_IFMT: 0x8000 file, 0x4000 directory,
                    0x2000 device. Low 12 bits are the mode: setuid 0x800,
                    owner rwx, other rwx, sticky 0x200. Shown as rwxrwxst.
                    chmod [u|o|a][+|-][rwxst] file; x follows u/o/a.
                    Owner or root only. Non-root cannot set setuid.
    0x02  i_uid     owner
    0x04  i_size    length in bytes
    0x14  i_dtime   deletion timestamp, non-zero on a deleted inode
    0x1a  i_links_count
    0x1c  i_blocks  sectors of 512 bytes, two per 1 KiB block
    0x28  i_block[15] twelve direct blocks, one single indirect, reserved

A directory is an ordinary file: its data is a run of variable-length
records, each an 8-byte header (ino@0, rec_len@4, name_len@6, file_type@7)
followed by the name, chained by rec_len up to i_size.

A login shell runs with the uid of its account; uid 0 bypasses every check,
and permission is checked against ownership, so /bin is off limits to
non-root users. block_read and block_write need the 'b' permission letter
(every account has it by default). A file is owned by the euid that created
it. /tmp is 1777 with the sticky bit, so accounts cannot delete each other's
scratch files; each /home/<name> directory belongs to its account, and mv
needs write permission on both directories. A check reads only the file's
own mode bits: a child may have any mode independently of its parent, while
parent modes still control traversal and directory-entry operations. PATH
searches /bin before /usr/bin, so a program there cannot shadow ls.

ls -l shows the mode, the owner, the size and the name of each entry. The
owner comes from i_uid: 0 prints as root, every other uid as a number. The
line is readview kind 8, so ls only needs search permission on the
directories along the way.

Both calls ls makes, getdents and readview kind 8, run entirely in the
CRX kernel; its read-only VFS walks inodes and directory blocks on any
device, crossing mount points through a table at 0x00C0 in the KCB.
ps, help, lsblk, dmesg and hexdump are formatted by that kernel too:
every table has text columns left aligned, numbers right aligned, and one
header row on top. A bare ls, ps or lsblk prints the short form; -a and
friends widen it with memory and filesystem details. objdump checks the
path itself, then asks the host to decode instructions.

    -rwxr-x--  root     262  as
    drwxrwx-t  root    1024  tmp/

## Where a file starts and ends

The block pointers say which blocks hold the file; they need not be
adjacent. i_size says how far into the last block the file runs. Nothing is
stored inline and no terminator is used: extent is the pointer list, end is
size. The CRX kernel writes the twelve direct blocks (12 KiB) and reads one
indirect level as well, so a file can be 12 + 256 = 268 blocks long; longer
files have to come from a real ext2 tool on the host side.

## Devices

    /dev/sda   system disk, 1024 blocks x 1 KiB (1 MiB), mounted at /
    /dev/sdb   first imported or created disk, then sdc, sdd, ...

Only sda exists at boot, holding the root tree and the installed programs;
a new disk takes the next free name. Where the system disk comes from is
chosen at boot — see Booting below.

## Mounting

    lsblk                    devices: name, size, type, mountpoint
    lsblk -d                 device details only
    lsblk -f                 filesystem usage only
    lsblk -a                 every device and filesystem field
    mount /dev/sdb /mnt      attach the device to a directory
    cp /usr/man/README /mnt/      copy a file onto it
    umount /mnt              detach it

Any device can be mounted anywhere, several at once on different
directories; mounting over /bin or / is rejected by the checks above.
Unmount before removing a disk: the kernel refuses a busy device with
EBUSY. Writes past the end of a disk fail with ENOSPC, and a rename
across devices fails with EXDEV — use cp for that.

Before mounting, the host checks the geometry: block size, inode size and
count, bitmap and inode-table positions must match this machine, because
the CRX machine code holds those numbers as constants. A foreign layout is
refused rather than half-read.

## Booting

The system disk comes from one of three places, chosen at boot:

    from IndexedDB          the sda saved in this browser, files and edits intact
    from a .img file        an exported image as the system disk; must be ext2
                            with this machine's geometry
    create an empty disk    format a fresh one and write the factory tree

All three re-install /bin at power-on so the programs match the current
firmware, and all three re-attach the removable disks stored in IndexedDB.
Without IndexedDB (headless runs, private mode) nothing is persisted but the
machine runs exactly the same.

## Writeback

There is no sync command. The kernel tracks a dirty flag and flushes
modified devices to IndexedDB about once a second, on unmount, on panic and
on shutdown; the Storage panel shows the current state. A disk is stored as
16 KiB chunks and a flush only writes the chunks whose contents changed —
an all-zero chunk is no different from a fresh disk and is not stored at
all. sync(2) still exists as call 17.

## Host transfer

The Storage panel saves a device as <name>.img, a byte-for-byte copy with
the superblock first, so sda exports as sda.img — a real ext2 image that
e2fsck -fn, debugfs and loop mounts accept on Linux. Importing never
overwrites a device: the image attaches as the next free name, and
mismatching images (wrong magic, different block size) are refused. An
exported sda.img can also come back as the system disk at the next boot
with "from a .img file".
